#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069942 :tutorial/content "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since \n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x.$\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.$ Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.$\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,$ but for cube roots, we have $\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.$ No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ad1bc727-9054-5115-83f0-a82aa24dc35e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1605 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
